  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The second-stage analysis of policy determinants relies on a cross-sectional design across 22 cities, which limits the ability to establish strict causal relationships.",
      "what": "Using quarterly data from 1988 to 2020 across 22 Finnish cities, this study estimates that long-run housing supply stock elasticity ranges from 0.3 to 2.2. By constructing novel city-level indices of land-use policy restrictiveness, the analysis shows that cities with more restrictive planning interventions, particularly those imposing higher indirect development costs, exhibit lower supply elasticities. Together with city size and geographic constraints, these policy factors explain approximately 70% of the variation in housing supply elasticity across the studied municipalities.",
      "why": "The study provides empirical evidence linking specific local planning delays and indirect development costs to reduced housing supply responsiveness, demonstrating that municipal land-use policies directly shape market elasticity even within a single national regulatory framework."
    }
